            <ArticleTitle>Stenting for coronary intervention-related dissection of the left main coronary artery with extension to the aortic root: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1165-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/SMJ.0b013e31817fe2f9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Retrograde aortocoronary dissection is a rare but devastating complication of coronary angioplasty. It occurs most frequently in the right coronary artery, rarely in the left. This is a case report of an aortic dissection complicated by coronary angioplasty of the left circumflex artery. Stenting of the left main coronary artery successfully sealed the entry point of dissection.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
